import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Settings",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's settings: environment variables (Env.var!), where the app is (Env.public_url!), the app's context made once at start from them, and Env.dev! for what only a developer's machine should do.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "env", label: "Settings: Env" },
		{ id: "context", label: "Read once: the app's context" },
		{ id: "dev", label: "Only while developing" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · settings"
				title="Settings from the environment"
			>
				An app's secrets, and whatever differs from one machine to the next, come from its environment
				variables, as in a twelve-factor app. This page shows how to read them, where the app is reachable,
				and how to do something only on a developer's machine. What an app does with its secrets (signing
				links, sealing what it stores) is on the <a href={Paths.platform_crypto}>crypto</a> page.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="env">Settings: Env</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_settings.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_settings.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>Env.var!(name)</code> reads one of the server's environment variables: <code>Ok(value)</code>,
					or <code>Err(NotFound)</code>. It works the same in a request, a job or a schedule, so a secret is
					read where the code that needs it is.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <code>.env</code> file in the directory the server starts in is read into its environment as it
					starts, before anything else: <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-…</code> there is read by
					<code>Env.var!</code>, and by every plugin, as if the shell had exported it. A variable the
					environment already has keeps its value, so a deployment's own settings win.
					<code>--env-file</code> (or <code>ROCX_ENV_FILE</code>) names another file; the tests' servers start with
					<code>ROCX_ENV_FILE</code> empty, so a developer's <code>.env</code> never reaches them. Lines are <code>NAME=value</code> (or <code>export
					NAME=value</code>), with <code>#</code> comments and quoted values; a line that isn't one stops the
					server, saying which. The log names what it set, never a value. Keep the file out of git.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Env.public_url!({"{}"})</code> is where the app is, for a link in an email or another
					server's callback: <code>--public-url</code> (or <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>) when the server was
					started with one, such as the proxy in front of it (<code>https://status.example.com</code>).
					Otherwise it is where the server listens, with the port it bound (including the one the system
					picked for <code>--port 0</code>). It is never taken from a request's <code>Host</code> header,
					which whoever asks can write.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The platform's own settings are its flags, each with an environment variable:
				<code>DATABASE_URL</code>, <code>PORT</code>, <code>MAX_BODY</code> and the rest
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running it</a>). A package's settings are read by the
				app, or by its plugin: <code>STORAGE_URL</code> for <a href={Paths.ecosystem_storage}>the storage
				package</a>, <code>SMTP_URL</code> for <a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>email</a>. A plugin that
				needs a variable says so, and the server won't start without it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="context">Read once: the app's context</Heading>
			<p>
				A setting every request needs, and that never changes while the server runs, is best read once.
				The app's <code>init!</code> runs when the server starts (after the migrations, so it may also read
				the database) and makes the app's <strong>context</strong>. The host keeps it, and gives it to every
				request's middleware, every actor's turn and every plugin's call. If <code>init!</code> returns an
				<code>Err</code>, the server stops with that message:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started|\n\t\tmatch Env.var!(\"APP_SECRET\") {\n\t\t\tOk(secret) => Ok({ secret })\n\t\t\tErr(NotFound) => Err(\"APP_SECRET isn't set\")\n\t\t},\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>init!</code> is given the text of the file named by <code>--context-file</code> (empty
				without one): a way to hand the app facts at start that aren't environment variables. This site
				keeps its build's facts that way. How middleware passes the context on to pages and endpoints is
				on the <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>routing</a> page.
			</p>

			<Heading id="dev">Only while developing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Env.dev!({"{}"})</code> says whether the server was started with <code>--dev</code>, as
				<code>rocx dev</code> and <code>just dev</code> start it. Use it for what only a developer's machine
				should do, like the "sign in as anyone" shortcut above. Under <code>--dev</code> the server also
				reloads open pages when it restarts, shows a crash's reason in the answer, and, with the email
				plugin and no <code>SMTP_URL</code>, keeps the email it would send and shows it at
				<code>/__rocx/mail</code> (<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_email}#mailbox"}>the dev mailbox</a>).
				<code>Env.debug!({"{}"})</code> says whether it was started with <code>--debug</code>, as tests start
				it: the email plugin keeps mail for the tests to read then too. With neither flag, as a deployed
				server runs, a server with the email plugin and no <code>SMTP_URL</code> doesn't start, rather
				than lose the email; nor does one whose app or plugins have tables and no database.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
